{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"No statewide rent control; municipal rent control authorized","requirement":"New Jersey has no state laws that establish, govern or control rents. Municipalities may adopt rent control or rent leveling ordinances, which locally created boards enforce.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Residential rentals statewide; whether a unit is covered depends on whether the local municipality has adopted a rent control or rent leveling ordinance.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":"HUD-owned or subsidized developments and projects financed by the New Jersey Housing and Mortgage Finance Agency are not subject to municipal rent control.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J. Dep't of Cmty. Affairs, Landlord-Tenant Information (Rent Control/Rent Increases)","quoted_span":"The State of New Jersey has no laws that establish, govern or control rents. Municipalities may pass an ordinance establishing rent control or rent leveling.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Newly constructed multiple dwelling rent control exemption","requirement":"Newly constructed multiple dwellings are exempt from any local rent control ordinance for 30 years after construction is completed; government-subsidized rents may also be exempt.","key_value":"30 years from completion of construction","coverage_conditions":"Newly constructed multiple dwellings; the text also notes that rents subsidized by governmental funding may be exempt from local rent control ordinances.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":"construction_date","min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"newly constructed multiple dwellings"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:42-84.5","quoted_span":"newly constructed multiple dwellings shall be exempt from any local rent control ordinances for a period of 30 years following completion of construction of the building.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Rent increase procedure and unconscionability standard","requirement":"A landlord cannot raise rent mid-lease: the old lease must be terminated and a new lease offered with written notice. Any increase must not be unconscionable and must comply with municipal rent ordinances.","key_value":"increase must not be unconscionable","coverage_conditions":"All residential tenants; a tenant may challenge an increase before a municipal rent control board or in court.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"The increase in rent must not be unconscionable; it must not be so unreasonable as to shock the conscience of a fair and honest person and must comply with any municipal ordinances governing rent increases.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"No rent increase to cover conversion costs","requirement":"When a building is converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership, rents may not be increased to cover costs resulting solely from the conversion.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All tenants in a building converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership, whether or not they qualify for protected tenancy.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":"Increases to cover the cost of new services or amenities are permitted.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.31","quoted_span":"If a building is converted to a condominium or cooperative form of ownership, or to fee simple ownership of units, rents may not be increased to cover cos ts resulting solely from the conversion (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.31).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Anti-Eviction Act good cause requirement","requirement":"A landlord may recover possession of a residential unit only with the tenant's consent or through the legal eviction process, and may not evict or refuse to renew a lease without good cause.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Residential dwelling units used as a residence; the text states no residential landlord may evict or fail to renew without good cause.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"residential dwelling units"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"No residential landlord may evict or fail to renew a lease, whether it is a written or an oral lease without good cause.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Notice to Quit and Notice to Cease requirements","requirement":"Each good cause for eviction, except nonpayment of rent, must be described in detail in a written notice to the tenant, and a Notice to Quit is required; a Notice to Cease may also be needed.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All good cause evictions of residential tenants; the text states no statutory time period for a Notice to Cease, which must be reasonable.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"residential tenancies"},"exemptions":"An eviction for nonpayment of rent does not require a Notice to Quit.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.2","quoted_span":"Each cause, except for nonpayment of rent, must be described in detail by the landlord in a written notice to the tenant.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"security_deposits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"New owner duty for security deposits on sale or transfer","requirement":"When rental property is sold or transferred, the new owner must obtain the tenant's security deposit and accrued interest and is responsible for investing it, giving notices, paying interest and returning it.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Residential rental property that is sold or transferred while a security deposit is held, whether or not the deposit and interest are actually transferred.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"rental property that is sold or transferred"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 46:8-21","quoted_span":"If the property is sold or transferred it is the duty of the new owner to obtain the security deposit, plus accrued interest on the tenant’s deposit, that was collected by the former owner.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"security_deposits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Penalty for withholding deposit of a tenant receiving public assistance","requirement":"A landlord who willfully and intentionally withholds a security deposit from a tenant receiving state or federal financial assistance is liable for a civil penalty of $500 to $2,000 per offense.","key_value":"$500-$2,000 per offense","coverage_conditions":"Tenants who have received financial assistance through any State or federal program, including welfare or rental assistance.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"tenant receives state or federal financial assistance"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"Civil penalty of not less than $500 or not more than $2,000 for each offense, enforced by summary proceeding; the State entity that made the deposits receives any penalty recovered.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 46:8-21.1","quoted_span":"The landlord may be liable for a civil penalty of not less than $500 or not more than $2,000 for each offense.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"security_deposits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Criminal penalty for unlawful use of security deposit monies","requirement":"A person who unlawfully uses security deposit monies may be charged as a disorderly person and fined at least $200 or imprisoned up to 30 days, or both.","key_value":"$200 fine; up to 30 days","coverage_conditions":"Any person who unlawfully uses security deposit monies.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"Disorderly person charge with a fine of not less than $200, imprisonment for not more than 30 days, or both.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 46:8-25","quoted_span":"may be subject to a fine of not less than $200 or imprisonment for not more than 30 days, or both (N.J.S.A. 46:8-25).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"screening_restrictions","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Law Against Discrimination source-of-income protection","requirement":"The New Jersey Law Against Discrimination bars landlords and rental agencies from refusing to rent or discriminating in housing based on source of lawful income, among other protected characteristics.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All landlord-tenant relationships in the State, except two-family owner-occupied dwellings, rooms in an owner- or resident-occupied single home, and single-sex or age-restricted housing as to familial status.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":true,"other":"all landlord-tenant relationships"},"exemptions":"Two-family owner-occupied dwellings; rooms in an owner- or resident-occupied single home; residences planned for and occupied by one sex and age-restricted housing (familial status).","penalty":"Fines and penalties under the LAD of up to $10,000 for a first offense; a violating landlord may also owe monetary damages and be ordered to rent the unit.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 10:5-12","quoted_span":"prohibits discrimination when selling or renting property and requires equal treatment in the sale or rental of housing regardless of race, creed, color, national origin, ancestry, sex, marital status, civil union status, domestic partnership status, familial status, affectional or sexual orientation, gender  identity or expression, mental and physical disability, nationality, or source of lawful income.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"application_screening_fees","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Landlord may charge tenant for screening report and application fees","requirement":"A landlord may charge a prospective tenant for the cost of a credit or tenant screening report and may request reasonable rental application fees or employment verification.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Applicants for residential rental units; the landlord may use the report in deciding whether to approve or deny the applicant.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J. Dep't of Cmty. Affairs, Landlord-Tenant Information (Credit Checks and Background Checks)","quoted_span":"The landlord is allowed to charge the tenant for the cost of the report. The landlord may also request reasonable rental application fees or employment verification.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
